//
// THE FALL — the feet stop being a function of the ground and become a STATE.
//
// Until now the editor set `py = terrain_y(px, pz, wld)` every tick: the feet were
// *derived* from the ground, so stepping off a ledge teleported you down and there
// was nothing a cliff could let you do that walking could not. A fall is what makes
// the cliff's asymmetry observable, which is why `cliff.loft` named it as the
// trigger for giving a cliff a direction.
//
// ── the invariant ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// > **The feet are never below the ground, and above it only while falling.**
//
// One rule, and it covers both directions: walking UP a ramp is the ground rising
// past the feet, which the same clause resolves by putting them on it. So there is
// no separate "climb" case — climbing is falling's other side.
//
// ⚠ **THIS MUST BE THE ONLY THING THAT EVOLVES `py`.** The editor had **nine**
// writers of it before this existed, each re-deriving the feet from the ground, and
// a fall omitted at any one of them would be silent — you would simply not fall in
// that situation. So the rule is: a *teleport* may set the feet authoritatively; a
// *tick* may only ever call this.
//
// ── ⚠ IT MOVED HERE, AND THE GRAVITY IS THE REASON IT WAITED ──────────────────
//
// **Plan 22 `B1c.3`.** This was `moros_sim`'s, and the editor's page could not reach
// it: `hex_editor` is a lavition package, so depending on a Moros one points
// [LAVITION_SPLIT](../../../doc/claude/LAVITION_SPLIT.md)'s arrow backwards. The walk
// arrived here at `B1c.2b`; a walker that cannot fall is only half of one.
//
// ⚠ **AND IT DID NOT MOVE SOONER BECAUSE OF ITS CONSTANTS**, which is worth stating
// exactly, because the note it replaces was right. `moros_sim::player` falls too —
// `player_step` applies its own `GRAVITY`, clamps to its own `TERMINAL_VELOCITY`,
// and lands through `collide::resolve_move` — and this file used to IMPORT those,
// under the sentence *"a package with two gravities is a package where a jump and a
// fall disagree"*. It was caught the honest way: this file was first written with a
// `GRAVITY_DEFAULT` of 11.0 invented from a figure's height, beside a `GRAVITY` of
// 12.0 that already shipped three modules away.
//
// ⚠ **WHAT CHANGED IS THAT THEY ARE NOW TWO PACKAGES, NOT ONE.** `player_step` moves
// a body against a **`Map`** (`moros_map`'s world model, still live — `editor.loft`
// drives it); this resolves against a ground height in a **`VoxelWorld`**. That
// representation split is `HEX_STACK.md` §4's, and the old note already named its
// own end: *"when the two world models converge, one of these goes."* Until then a
// second declaration for a second world model is honest; a second declaration for
// the SAME one never was.
//
// ⚠ **SO THE NUMBERS ARE PINNED AT BOTH ENDS AND THE PIN IS A TEST, NOT AN IMPORT.**
// `tests/fall.loft` asserts 12.0 and 60.0 here; `moros_sim/tests/skeleton.loft`
// asserts 12.0 there; and each file names the other. ⚠ That is weaker than sharing a
// constant and it is said so rather than dressed up: **nothing makes them equal — a
// test on each side makes a drift LOUD.**
//
// ⚠ **AND THEY ARE `FALL_*`, NOT `GRAVITY`.** `src/editor_server.loft` imports this
// package AND `moros_sim`, so a bare `GRAVITY` published here is a name declared by
// two packages in one program. A public name claims a word in every consumer's
// namespace — `WALK_SKIN` was that lesson one step ago, learned from a collision
// rather than before one.

// The editor's gravity, in world units per second squared, and the speed past which
// a longer drop stops arriving faster.
//
// ⚠ THE CLAMP IS NOT A REFINEMENT: without it a deep enough fall steps THROUGH thin
// ground between two ticks.
pub const FALL_GRAVITY = 12.0;
pub const FALL_TERMINAL = 60.0;

pub struct Fall {
  fl_y: float,             // the feet, after the step
  fl_vy: float,            // vertical speed, negative downward
  fl_landed: boolean,      // this step is the one that touched down
  fl_airborne: boolean     // still in the air after this step
}

// One step of the feet toward the ground under them.
//
// Semi-implicit Euler — velocity first, then position. It is the stable order for a
// constant acceleration, and it is what makes the landing test below exact rather
// than approximately right: the position used for the ground test is the one the
// new velocity produced.
pub fn fall_step(y: float, vy: float, gnd: float, dt: float,
                 g: float = FALL_GRAVITY) -> Fall {
  // Already at or under the surface: stand on it. This is also the CLIMB case —
  // the ground rose past the feet while walking up a ramp — and it needs no
  // separate branch, which is the whole point of stating the invariant this way.
  if y <= gnd {
    return Fall { fl_y: gnd, fl_vy: 0.0, fl_landed: false, fl_airborne: false };
  }
  nvy = vy - g * dt;
  // The same clamp `moros_sim::player_step` applies, at the same value — see the
  // header for why that is now two constants rather than one import.
  if nvy < 0.0 - FALL_TERMINAL { nvy = 0.0 - FALL_TERMINAL; }
  ny = y + nvy * dt;
  if ny <= gnd {
    // Crossed the surface within this step: land on it exactly. The overshoot is
    // discarded rather than bounced — a walker does not bounce — and the landing
    // is REPORTED so a caller can make a noise about it.
    return Fall { fl_y: gnd, fl_vy: 0.0, fl_landed: true, fl_airborne: false };
  }
  Fall { fl_y: ny, fl_vy: nvy, fl_landed: false, fl_airborne: true }
}

// How far the feet are above what is under them. Zero standing, positive falling —
// and never negative, which is the invariant as a number a test can read.
pub fn fall_clearance(y: float, gnd: float) -> float {
  d = y - gnd;
  if d < 0.0 { return 0.0; }
  d
}
